%% P02 parent/child timeline for run1 (Linux). The only guaranteed ordering is: child exit happens before parent's wait() returns.
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor U as Me (Terminal A)
    participant S as bash (shell)
    participant P as parent.py (PID 521)
    participant K as Kernel
    participant C as child.py (PID 525)
    participant F as output/child-result.txt

    U->>S: python3 starter/parent.py
    S->>K: start python3 (shell fork + exec, not traced)
    K-->>P: new process, runs parent.py
    P->>K: Popen() - vfork + execve(python3 -u child.py), as seen in the strace run
    K-->>C: new process PID 525, PPID 521
    P->>K: wait4(525) - parent BLOCKED (wchan do_wait)
    C->>C: print PID and PPID, then CPU phase 0.75 s (user mode, Running/Ready)
    C->>K: read(0) via input() - child BLOCKED (wchan wait_woken)
    Note over U,C: Terminal B inspects PID 525 with ps and /proc while both wait
    U->>K: press Enter (terminal input)
    K-->>C: read returns "\n" - child Ready, then Running
    C->>K: openat / write(114 bytes) / close
    K->>F: synthetic labels saved (page cache)
    C->>K: exit_group(7)
    K-->>P: wait4 returns status 7 - child reaped
    P->>U: "wait() returned - child exit code was 7"
    P->>K: exit_group(0)
